  "worked_solution": "First, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{\u2032}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}({s}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061s) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}({s}^{2})\\operatorname{ln}\u2061s+{s}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061s) \\\\  & =(2s)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}\u2061s+{s}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{s}) \\\\  & =2s\\operatorname{ln}\u2061s+s \\\\  & =s(2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061s+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate change of the surface area is $s(2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061s+1)$ squared millimeters per centimeter.",
